>
  <!-- Lead In Paragraph -->
  <p class="text-lg text-gray-950 font-semibold mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    The <strong>Chevy Equinox EV</strong> has rapidly cemented its position as America's benchmark value electric crossover. Starting under $35,000 before the $7,500 federal clean vehicle tax credit, it brings 300+ miles of EPA-rated range within reach of everyday American households. But for prospective buyers cross-shopping against traditional gas compact SUVs, the decisive financial question is: <strong>How much does it actually cost per month to charge a Chevy Equinox EV at home versus filling up with gasoline or using commercial fast chargers?</strong>
  </p>

  <p class="mb-8 leading-relaxed">
    Equipped with General Motors' Ultium powertrain architecture featuring an <strong>85.0 kWh usable battery pack</strong>, the Equinox EV delivers remarkable energy efficiency for a roomy five-seater crossover. In this authoritative 2026 empirical guide, the <strong>VoltSavings Research Team</strong> breaks down the real-world kilowatt-hour (kWh) math for home Level 2 charging, evaluates off-peak Time-of-Use (TOU) utility rates across major states, compares Tesla Supercharger costs using GM's official NACS adapter, and calculates your multi-year net cash savings versus a 28 MPG gas SUV.
  </p>

  <!-- Key Takeaways Highlight Box -->
  <div class="my-8 p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-emerald-50 via-teal-50/40 to-emerald-50 border-2 border-emerald-300 rounded-3xl not-prose shadow-sm">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
      <span class="w-7 h-7 rounded-lg bg-emerald-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-black">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-bolt"></i>
      </span>
      <h3 class="text-sm font-black uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-950">Key Takeaways: 2026 Equinox EV Charging Economics</h3>
    </div>
    <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-800 font-medium">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-check text-emerald-600 mt-1 shrink-0"></i>
        <span><strong>Full 0–100% Home Charge (US National Avg @ 16.8¢/kWh):</strong> Costs approximately <strong>$15.71</strong> (replenishing 85 kWh with 10% AC charging loss = 93.5 kWh from the wall).</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-check text-emerald-600 mt-1 shrink-0"></i>
        <span><strong>Overnight Off-Peak Tariff (8.0¢/kWh):</strong> A complete overnight charge drops to just <strong>$7.48</strong>, delivering over 300 miles of highway range.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-check text-emerald-600 mt-1 shrink-0"></i>
        <span><strong>Monthly Commute Cost (1,000 Miles):</strong> Costs roughly <strong>$52.80/month</strong> at home (<strong>~5.2¢/mile</strong>) versus <strong>$125.00/month</strong> for a 28 MPG gas SUV at $3.50/gal (<strong>12.5¢/mile</strong>)—saving <strong>$72.20 every month ($866/year)</strong>.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation text-amber-600 mt-1 shrink-0"></i>
        <span><strong>Public DC Fast Charging &amp; Superchargers (45¢–54¢/kWh):</strong> A typical 10% to 80% highway top-up costs <strong>$27 to $32</strong> (<strong>13¢ to 15¢/mile</strong>). To maximize savings, do 85%+ of your charging at home.</span>
