  <!-- Section 1 -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    1. Chevy Equinox EV Battery Specs &amp; Charging Hardware Architecture
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    To compute electrical charging costs with surgical precision, one must inspect the vehicle's underlying battery chemistry and internal inverter architecture. General Motors built the Equinox EV upon its modular Ultium platform, employing a 10-module nickel-cobalt-manganese-aluminum (NCMA) pouch cell configuration:
  </p>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 my-8 not-prose">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <span class="text-[10px] font-black uppercase text-gray-400 block mb-1">Usable Capacity</span>
      <strong class="text-xl font-black text-gray-950 block">85.0 kWh</strong>
      <p class="text-[11px] text-gray-600 mt-1">Gross capacity ~89 kWh with ~4 kWh software safety buffer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <span class="text-[10px] font-black uppercase text-gray-400 block mb-1">EPA Rated Range</span>
      <strong class="text-xl font-black text-emerald-700 block">319 Miles</strong>
      <p class="text-[11px] text-gray-600 mt-1">Single-Motor FWD (213 hp). Dual-motor eAWD delivers 285 miles (288 hp).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <span class="text-[10px] font-black uppercase text-gray-400 block mb-1">Level 2 AC Onboard</span>
      <strong class="text-xl font-black text-gray-950 block">11.5 kW</strong>
      <p class="text-[11px] text-gray-600 mt-1">Adds up to 34 miles/hour on a 240V / 48A dedicated home circuit.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <span class="text-[10px] font-black uppercase text-gray-400 block mb-1">DC Fast Charging</span>
      <strong class="text-xl font-black text-gray-950 block">150 kW Peak</strong>
      <p class="text-[11px] text-gray-600 mt-1">10% to 80% replenishment in approximately 30 minutes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    In routine real-world commuting—combining suburban secondary roads, urban stop-and-go with one-pedal regenerative braking, and 70 mph interstate cruising—the front-wheel-drive Equinox EV averages approximately <strong>3.5 miles per kilowatt-hour (mi/kWh)</strong>, which translates to an energy consumption rate of <strong>28.5 kWh per 100 miles</strong>. During harsh freezing winter conditions with cabin heat running, consumption climbs to roughly 2.8 mi/kWh (~35.7 kWh/100 mi), while pleasant spring temperatures yield up to 3.8 to 4.0 mi/kWh.
  </p>

  <!-- Section 2 -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    2. Home Charging Cost Breakdown: Full Charge (0–100%) vs Daily Commute (10–80%)
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    A critical engineering detail frequently overlooked by simplistic online calculators is <strong>Level 2 charging efficiency loss</strong>. During AC-to-DC conversion within the vehicle's onboard inverter and thermal management circulation, roughly 8% to 12% of electrical energy is dissipated as heat. To deliver 85.0 kWh of chemical energy into the battery pack, the residential utility meter must register approximately <strong>93.5 kWh of electricity</strong> (assuming 90% charging efficiency).
  </p>
  <p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    Furthermore, lithium-ion battery preservation best practices recommend daily charging to <strong>80% state-of-charge (SoC)</strong>, reserving 100% full charges for long-distance highway road trips. A routine 10% to 80% charge window replenishes <strong>59.5 kWh of usable battery energy</strong>, drawing approximately <strong>65.4 kWh from your wall outlet</strong>.
  </p>

  <!-- Detailed Cost Table -->
