          <td class="p-3.5 font-bold text-amber-900">$22.25</td>
          <td class="p-3.5 font-bold text-amber-900">$31.79</td>
          <td class="p-3.5 font-black text-amber-950">$97.20 / mo</td>
          <td class="p-3.5 font-bold text-amber-900">9.72¢ / mi</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 italic mb-8">
    *Calculations factor in 10% AC Level 2 charging losses (93.5 kWh wall draw for 100% capacity; 65.4 kWh wall draw for 10–80% top-up) at real-world baseline efficiency of 3.5 mi/kWh.
  </p>

  <!-- Section 3 -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    3. Public DC Fast Charging &amp; Tesla Supercharger Costs (with NACS Adapter)
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    One of the greatest competitive advantages of owning a GM electric vehicle in 2026 is seamless access to the <strong>Tesla Supercharger network</strong>. Using an approved GM NACS-to-CCS1 adapter, Equinox EV owners can tap into thousands of reliable V3 (250 kW) and V4 Supercharger stalls nationwide. You can initiate and pay for charging sessions directly through the myChevrolet mobile app or the Tesla app.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    However, public fast charging carries a substantial financial premium over domestic home charging:
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-800 font-medium">
    <li><strong>Tesla Superchargers (Non-Tesla Member Rate):</strong> Typically averages <strong>$0.42 to $0.52 per kWh</strong> across interstate corridors. A 10% to 80% road trip top-up (59.5 kWh) costs between <strong>$25.00 and $31.00</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Electrify America &amp; EVgo (Standard PAYG):</strong> Averages <strong>$0.48 to $0.56 per kWh</strong> (plus potential session connection fees in select municipalities). The same 10% to 80% charge costs <strong>$28.50 to $33.30</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Highway Cost Per Mile:</strong> Driving exclusively on public fast chargers pushes your operating cost to approximately <strong>13.5¢ to 15.5¢ per mile</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Section 4 -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    4. Head-to-Head: Chevy Equinox EV vs Gas Chevy Equinox (28 MPG)
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    To understand the true total cost of ownership (TCO) advantage, let us compare the <strong>Equinox EV</strong> against its combustion counterpart, the traditional <strong>Chevy Equinox 1.5L Turbo gas SUV</strong> delivering an EPA combined rating of 28 US MPG. We model an American commuter driving <strong>12,000 miles per year (1,000 miles per month)</strong> with gasoline priced at $3.50 per gallon:
  </p>

  <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8 bg-white rounded-2xl border-2 border-gray-200 not-prose shadow-sm">
    <table class="w-full text-xs text-left border-collapse">
      <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-950 font-black uppercase text-[11px] border-b border-gray-200">
