  <p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    Over a five-year ownership span, an Equinox EV driver charging at home preserves between <strong>$5,100 and $6,800 in unburned fuel and maintenance expenditure</strong> compared to the gas Equinox. When stacked on top of the <strong>$7,500 Federal Clean Vehicle Tax Credit</strong> for qualifying buyers, the total financial benefit comfortably eclipses $12,500.
  </p>

  <!-- Section 5 -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    5. Equinox EV vs Tesla Model Y: Charging Curve &amp; Efficiency Compared
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Prospective American EV buyers invariably compare the Chevy Equinox EV against the country's best-selling electric crossover, the Tesla Model Y. In our in-depth <a href="/blog/tesla-model-y-home-charging-cost-us" class="text-emerald-700 font-bold underline">Tesla Model Y Home Charging Cost Breakdown</a> and flagship <a href="/blog/ev-charging-cost-us-2026" class="text-emerald-700 font-bold underline">US EV Charging Cost Guide</a>, we documented that the Model Y operates at a slightly higher highway efficiency of approximately 3.8 mi/kWh versus 3.5 mi/kWh for the Equinox EV.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    However, the Equinox EV counters with a larger battery reserve (<strong>85 kWh usable</strong> vs ~75 kWh usable in the Model Y Long Range) and a very consistent Level 2 AC onboard charging speed of 11.5 kW. While the Model Y charges faster at peak DC fast charging speeds (up to 250 kW on V3 Superchargers vs 150 kW for the Equinox), the Equinox EV holds its 100 kW+ charge rate remarkably well through 60% battery capacity. For drivers conducting 90% of their charging at home overnight, the day-to-day electrical bill delta between the two vehicles is under $4 to $6 per month.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-6 tracking-tight">
    Frequently Asked Questions (Chevy Equinox EV Charging)
  </h2>

  <div class="space-y-4 my-6 not-prose">
    <details class="group bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden cursor-pointer shadow-xs hover:border-emerald-400 transition-all" open>
      <summary class="flex justify-between items-center text-sm font-black text-gray-950">
        <span>How much does it cost to fully charge a Chevy Equinox EV at home?</span>
        <span class="text-emerald-600 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"><i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down"></i></span>
      </summary>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3 font-medium">
        At the US national average residential electricity rate of $0.168 per kWh, fully charging the Equinox EV's 85 kWh usable battery (drawing ~93.5 kWh from the wall with 10% AC charging loss) costs approximately $15.71. On an off-peak night tariff ($0.08/kWh), a complete recharge costs just $7.48.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="group bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden cursor-pointer shadow-xs hover:border-emerald-400 transition-all">
      <summary class="flex justify-between items-center text-sm font-black text-gray-950">
        <span>How many hours does it take to charge an Equinox EV on Level 2?</span>
        <span class="text-emerald-600 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"><i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down"></i></span>
      </summary>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3 font-medium">
        Using a standard 48-Amp Level 2 home wallbox operating at 11.5 kW, replenishing a typical 20% to 80% daily charge takes roughly 4.5 to 5.5 hours. A complete 0 to 100% recharge takes approximately 8.5 hours—easily accomplished overnight while you sleep.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="group bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden cursor-pointer shadow-xs hover:border-emerald-400 transition-all">
      <summary class="flex justify-between items-center text-sm font-black text-gray-950">
        <span>Can I plug a Chevy Equinox EV into a regular 120V household wall outlet?</span>
        <span class="text-emerald-600 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"><i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down"></i></span>
      </summary>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3 font-medium">
        Yes, Level 1 (120V) charging is supported via GM's dual-voltage cord set. However, Level 1 charging delivers only 1.4 kW, adding roughly 3 to 4 miles of range per hour. A full recharge from empty on 120V takes over 60 hours, making a dedicated 240V Level 2 circuit highly recommended for daily commuters.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="group bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden cursor-pointer shadow-xs hover:border-emerald-400 transition-all">
